er switches layers the  structure is reproduced with high  fidelity as the transition between one  material and another does not introduce  start/stop errors that could accumulate  in tall structures with many material  transitions in addition the build time  does not depend on the number of  switching events this approach can be  used to recreate the banded smooth  muscle pattern seen in the human airway  for example in the center we show that  with on-the-fly computer coordination of  on-chip processes within the printer  tool path we can create coaxial  structures with clean boundaries and  demonstrate coordinated intra layer  material switching this structure can be  used to model the various concentric  layers of a tissue like a blood vessel  or the intesting on the right hand side  we demonstrate interweaving one hydrogel  within a solid and thin film of a second  hydrogel this capability can be used as  the basis of a vascularized skin or  tissue patch these structures reproduce  the intended geometry with high fidelity  and they're mechanically robust post  printing so we see an initial  opportunity in developing bioprinted  tissues that could be used at the  preclinical stages of drug development  now I'll briefly go over a few example  3d tissue models for use in preclinical  icings  so the loan is an incredibly complex  branching network of tubes each tube  ends in one of 300 million alveoli per  lung where gas exchange occurs now even  though we can bio-print with fine  control over macro and micro structures  we can't print something as complex as  the human lung yet but we can print some  of the components of the lung in a way  that recreates lifelike function so this  is a fluorescent image of the bands of  smooth muscle cells that wrap around the  airway and it's these muscles that  contract if you have an asthma attack we  decided to model this muscle tissue in  vitro by printing airway muscle cells  into 3d cube Euler structures that have  the ability to contract and we call this  tissue the 3d bio ring so it takes about  30 seconds to print a bio ring tissue  and post printing these bio printed  structures are living and so they have  to be matured and grown in specialized  culture conditions the bio printed  muscle cells are viable and over time  those cells form interconnected  three-dimensional networks and crucially  those printed tissues are able to  contract in a similar way to the real  human airway and so they demonstrate a  biological and disease relevance  function our goal was to use this tissue  to recreate an asthma attack in a dish  now the 3d bearing tackles the lack of  contractile models that mimic airway  biology at present the gold-standard  lab-based  airway contraction model is the  precision-cut lungs light  this is where 200 microns six sections  of living lung tissue are taken and  these tissues have a great advantage in  that they contain all of the relevant  cell types from the biological tissue  however disadvantages of the piece Els  model include limitations of tissue and  donor availability challenging  preparation protocols and a limited  lifespan in culture and all of these  really limit the broad adoption of this  assay aspects 3d bio ring airway is a 3d  bio printed tissue that contains primary  airway smooth muscle cells at  approximately 25 million cells per mil  these cells are viable in 3d culture and  the tissues are functional the bio ring  has a rapid dose-dependent contract our  response to histamine and a similar  relaxation response to the anti asthma  drug salbutamol cells from different  donors can be incorporated matrix  components can be added to manipulate  cellular microenvironment and tissue  production is rapid taking less than a  minute per tissue these tissues are  produced with very high repeatability if  you're interested in learning more than  you can find out on more on our website  where you can access our poster that was  presented in the American Thoracic  Society last year  so to validate buying tissue function we  use the dose response curves for  histamine and salbutamol to calculate  the sensitivity of the tissue to these  compounds the sensitivity is called the  EC 50 or the effective dose that gives  50 percent response and we compared the  EC 50s of the 3d bearings to publish  data for the same compounds in human and  guinea pig precision cut long slices  this comparison table demonstrates that  the bio ring has a physiologically  appropriate contraction response to  histamine and a pharmacologically  appropriate relaxation to salbutamol  suggesting our bioprinted bio ring  tissue is able to generate  physiologically relevant data similar to  the highly complex precision-cut lung  sliced issue assay  so confirming cell viability is always  important when dealing with bioprinted  tissues we've modified and optimized  viability assays specifically for 3d  tissues on the left you can see some  fluorescent images from a Cal scene am  propidium iodide assay this is a  fluorescent stain we're living cells are  shown in green and red cells dead cells  by counting these same cells we can  quantify the percentage of cell  viability in the healthy control image  you can see that the majority of the  cells here are green and almost 100% of  these cells are viable cells if we take  the same tissue and freeze Thorat and  -20 for a few hours to trigger cell  death then you can see that many of the  cells in this sample are red and  therefore dead in the center of the LDH  or lactate dehydrogenase assay this is  an assay where we can measure the  release of lactate dehydrogenase from  dead cells into the surrounding liquid  media the more LDH release the more dead  cells are present this assay can be  measured in a plate based format giving  us higher throughput compared to the  calcium a MPI assay on the right hand  side of the slide you can see data from  an alum are blue or reside urine  reduction assay here living cells reduce  resulting into a fluorescent form that's  released into the surrounding media hi  al Ahmar blue gives Hauser is it is  reflective of high cell viability and a  low signal is representative of low cell  viability as evidenced in the FreeStore  sample again this is suitable for a  plate based assay format so the lactate  dehydrogenase and Alamar blue assays can  be multiplexed to measure dead cells and  healthy cells simultaneously giving a  relative measure of cell viability in  relatively high throughput  so I gave one example of a functional  bioprinted tissue assay with the 3d bio  ring but the lab on a printer technology  is applicable to multiple tissue types  and applications these are two examples  of alternative tissues so in the left  hand side the the 3d liver is a bio  printed liver tissue and we demonstrate  that the hep g2 hepatocyte cell line  printed into 3d structures are viable  and able to grow we tested hypothesize  function using albumin release into the  surrounding media and measured that by  Eliza and we can demonstrate that the  printed tissues really maintain function  for at least 21 days post printing as  seen by extended release of albumin on  the right hand side we have a 3d cancer  model and this is where we can really  probe the interaction between tumor  cells in the surrounding stroma a  potentially vital interaction in the  regulation of tumor immunogenicity and  spreading so we've developed a  multicellular tissue design that  contains a core of tumor cells in this  case HeLa cells ovarian cancer cells  surrounded by healthy stromal cells in  this case dermal fibroblasts and using  this system we're able to investigate  physiological 3-dimensional tumor  stromal interactions in a very  controlled manner in vitro so we've  successfully printed a variety of  adherent primary human cells cell lines  and non-adherent immune cells in this  case cord blood derived mast cells all  of these cells that we tested  demonstrate high cell viability in our  systems and to some extent biologically  appropriate behavior including cell  spreading network formation or colony  formation  now our long-term goal this aspect is to  develop the most complex of tissues for  applications in regenerative medicine  and one application that we're focused  on is knee meniscus tissue for  transplantation the meniscus is a common  site of injury and has poor healing  properties while complete or partial  removal of the meniscus relieves acute  pain it leads to osteoarthritis now  we've partnered with a leading  orthopaedic company on a project to  develop 3d printed meniscus tissue for  implantation that will then graft into  the host tissue and provide a permanent  knee meniscus replacement now recreating  the heterogeneous orientation of fibers  and material composition within the  biological meniscus is essential for  achieving specific mechanical properties  such as tensile and compressive strength  in this video you will see how we're  able to precisely customize fiber  patterning and composition using aspects  proprietary software sweet cue video  you  you  so we're distributing our lab on a  printer technology broadly through the  launch of a commercial bio printing  system called the RX one and we work  with our partners and technology users  to provide a complete tissue fabrication  solution so in addition to the bio  printer platform itself we also supply  print heads bio-ink matrix and tissue  licenses these licenses are detailed  instruction manuals on how to print  culture and assay specific pre validated  tissues such as the 3d bio ring a key  part of our strategy at aspect is to  work closely with our academic partners  to drive forward the development of  impactful tissue applications this  includes access to technology and  know-how as well as supporting grant  applications for example we'll be  working with our initial early platform  access partners on applications ranging  from developing