oftware and so  that tissue is produced in a very  consistent and reliable way it's also  produced in an automated way  so you know we can produce many tissues  in sequence or even in parallel  sometimes to increase efficiency of that  tissue production  what are some advantages of the LLP bio  printing technology vs. other bio  printing methods so I mean I discussed  some of the various approaches to bio  printing and each approach really has  its own advantages and disadvantages  with the lab on a printer approach what  we've been able to do is really leverage  micro fluidics  to use a coaxial flow focusing  technology and so what this allows us to  do is generate a cell loaded hydrogel  fiber on chip but protect those cells  from the shear forces that they would  otherwise be exposed to in other types  of bio printing technologies so really  we believe that because there is very  little shear stress in our system we're  able to produce tissues with cells that  are at a very high viability and also  print at high speeds so we think we have  a very rapid tissue fabrication  technology as well you mentioned  customization of bio x how does this  work  so the the by links that we print can be  loaded with a variety of different  growth factors matrix factors really any  soluble bioactive compound and we supply  kind of basic bio inks to our to our  users and that's a broadly applicable  bio ink that's been validated with a  variety of different cell types but  sometimes there's a cell type that needs  a very sort of specific micro  environment in order to grow or  differentiate in the right way and so  what we're able to do is really  customize those bio inks by adding  specific components to to those inks to  really trigger the the correct cell  growth and differentiation of those  cells  has a tissue license work the tissue  license can be thought of as a detailed  instruction manual on how to how to  print a tissue so that includes  information on things like cell density  and cell type preparing cells and then  also printing parameters such as the  pressure settings on chip and then  moving through that kind of tissue  process we also supply information on  how to culture those tissues in the  appropriate manner and then also how to  say them so for example with the 3d  bearing tissue we provide detailed  information on how to perform the  contraction assays and even how to  quantify the data that comes out of  those assays so it's a sort of an  end-to-end tissue instruction manual how  do you validate the tissues you print  are functional the tissue validation  really depends on the type of tissue  that we're dealing with so for the the  contractile bio ring airway tissue what  we did what we did there was we tested  contraction in response to a variety of  compounds that were known previously to  cause contraction in human Airways so  histamine for example is well known to  cause airway contraction and so we used  that compound to test the contractile  response in our bio rings and did that  of various concentrations to calculate  the sensitivity of those rings and then  really compare that sensitivity to  pre-existing data that comes from their  other tissue models or even clinical  data and really it if you know as we  develop other tissues then they're  validated in very similar ways so it's  really using a panel of compounds with  known effect known clinical effects and  known preclinical effects  and then testing those in our system to  confirm that our tissues basically  reproduce those those known effects for  those compounds okay we have time for  one more question can you buy a print  samples using the LLP and yeah we  printed a variety of different cells  including primary human cells some of  those are kind of tricky to grow and  culture so we've definitely printed some  you know relatively fragile cell types  using this system and some of our  collaborators and our technology users  do have stem cell based projects that  they're focused on so for example one of  our users is is using induced  pluripotent stem cells and printing  those to derive neuronal tissues for  testing and researching  neurodegenerative diseases and testing  potential therapeutics for those  diseases so yeah we we're certainly  working with people who are interested  and using our system for for printing  stem cells I would like to once again  thank dr. Samuel watts for his first  presentation I would also like to thank  lab roots for making today's educational  webcast possible before we go  I want to let everyone know that today's  webcast will be available for on-demand  viewing through August 24 2017 you'll  receive an email from Lavery's letting  me know when this webcast will be  available for replay please share that  announcement with your colleagues who  may have missed today's live event  that's all for now thank you for joining  us we hope to see you again soon goodbye  thank you
 

